* staging is unnecessary complication of the version control system
* git
	* UI design is bad
	* main concepts to understand
		* DAG of nodes
		* nodes can be viewed as commits or changesets or snapshots
		* branches are [represented by] just pointers to nodes
		* "HEAD" is a pointer to a node or to a branch
			* when it points not to a branch then it is said to be "detached"
		* good video introduction about the basics
			* LearnThatStack: Git Will Finally Make Sense After This
				\ links-to external https://youtu.be/Ala6PHlYjmw?si=-hlAmC3f2Vzv5SQw
	* commands
		* command 'checkout' moves the HEAD pointer where nodes are commits or changesets or snapshots depending on how to and updates the working directory
		* command 'reset' moves HEAD 
			* if points to a branch [pointer] then that gets moved too
			* makes (the rest of the branch from the new HEAD position) "orphan"ed
			* versions
				* '--soft'
					* only changes the branch pointer, does not change the stage or the working dir
					* can be used for example to combine later changesets
				* '--mixed' [the default]
					* like 'soft' but also empties the stage
					* can be used for example to split a changeset
				* '--hard' [very dangerous]
					* like 'mixed' but also resets the working dir to the new HEAD position, hence loses all later changesets
			* in a 'git reset HEAD' "head" refers not to as what is moved (because it is always the HEAD that is moved) but as the target position of the movement
				* such command is used to destage files
					* more modern versions of git provide 'git restore --staged' for this purpose
						* which is still ugly IMO
					* the good UI design would be
						* git stage add    <file>
						* git stage remove <file>
		* command 'revert x' at HEAD creates a new commit which is the opposite of x
		* command 'rebase'
			* recreates given branch from given point
				* the newly created nodes
				* have equivalent [relatively to the position] content
				* new hash [as they are not the same nodes]
					* hence do not rebase public branch
		* command 'reflog' shows the orphaned nodes which are not yet garbage-collected
	* how to
		* edit a deep changeset
			* 'git rebase -i'
			* 'git reset HEAD~'
				* do not omit "~" from the end of "HEAD"
		* deleting history
			* to save storage cost for github hosting a website
			* this algorithm deletes all the history
				* take the storage of the history away from git
					* mv .git .git-backup
				* create the repository like it would not exist before
					* git init
				* get back the configuration of the repository
					* cp .git-backup/config .git/config
				* create a first commit in the new history
					* git add --all git commit -m "1"
				* push this whole renewal of the history to the server
					* git push -u --force origin master
				* delete folder .git-backup
			* to delete only part of the history is also possible
				\ links-to external https://stackoverflow.com/questions/41953300/how-to-delete-the-old-git-history
	* folder layout
		* "working tree" - where the versioned data is
			* configure in git config file under section "core" as 'worktree = ...'
		* "git directory" - were the git data is
			* it is normally the ".git" folder but when not then a ".git" file takes its place, in which configure as "gitdir: ..."
